<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19869116</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2131066</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE RELATION OF BACILLUS ABORTUS FROM BOVINE SOURCES TO MALTA FEVER</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>Theobald</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1c19450N0x377e890">From the Department of Animal Pathology of The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research, Princeton, N. J.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>31</day><month>1</month><year>1926</year></pub-date><volume>43</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>207</fpage><lpage>223</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>29</day><month>10</month><year>1925</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1926, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> 1. Comparative tests on guinea pigs with <italic>B. abortus</italic> yield the best results when minute doses are injected subcutaneously and the animal kept at least 4 weeks. 2. <italic>B. abortus</italic> gradually loses its virulence for guinea pigs under artificial cultivation. 3. Two cultures resembling <italic>B. abortus</italic> from cattle, isolated from human cases of so called Malta fever, are shown to be in their effect on guinea pigs not identical with the bovine strains. 4. The results of studies of <italic>B. abortus</italic> from swine indicate a close relation between the porcine strains and the two human strains. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>